Here’s how you can invoke the helper to compare the flags of your two declaration objects.  
Just replace `parameterObject` and `variableDeclarationObject` with the actual objects you’re working with (or their JSON‑stringified forms).

```json
{
  "left": "{\"kind\":\"Parameter\",\"modifiers\":[\"public\",\"readonly\"],\"optional\":true,\"name\":\"x\",\"type\":\"number\"}",
  "right": "{\"kind\":\"VariableStatement\",\"modifiers\":[\"private\",\"static\"],\"name\":\"y\",\"type\":\"number\"}"
}
```

If you prefer to call it directly from JavaScript/TypeScript:

```ts
import { areDeclarationFlagsIdentical } from 'your-toolbox';

const leftObj = /* your parameter object */;
const rightObj = /* your variable declaration object */;

// Convert to string representations if needed
const leftStr = JSON.stringify(leftObj);
const rightStr = JSON.stringify(rightObj);

const identical = areDeclarationFlagsIdentical({
  left: leftStr,
  right: rightStr
});

console.log(identical); // true or false
```

The function internally checks all relevant flags—optionality, privacy/protection, async, abstract, readonly, static, etc.—and returns `true` only when they match exactly (with the special allowance for optionality differences).
